  -> CALIBRATION FIRST, and it is exact rather than referential: V has two independent expressions -- Var_{p_T}(z), and T^3 dH/dT by numerical differentiation of the entropy curve -- which are equal analytically. Worst relative disagreement across every model and context measured is 2.30e-03 against a tolerance of 0.02. The implementation reproduces the identity, so V is what the derivation says it is. PRIMARY: over 14 models with a finite T*, the heat-capacity peak and the measured melting temperature correlate at rho = -0.692, mean |T_V - T*| = 1.0351. correlation_leverage: heat-capacity peak T_V spans 0.6012 against the target's 0.3284 (ratio 1.83, gate 0.5); a correlation is interpretable; distinct_units: 14 sampled T_V values collapse to 11 distinct (gate 4) The peak does NOT land on T*: T* measures something the static conditional does not contain, so F86's anchor is not a restatement of next-token entropy. This is the outcome that protects T*, and it is only worth having because the deflationary one was written down first as a live possibility.

